Read orbitals and electron configurations as quantum models (@gc-quantum: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #general-chemistry "Periodic-patterns-and-their-limits.lingua#r_1JPAXST3A4ZTBAZQWJKCJR8AZW") { r_4B0D7XPCFSZAW46VKG2NE7B54P
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 6 in full with exercises; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.112, Fall 2005](../sources/MIT-5-112.lingua#r_05VDAXGTY0M9E5K9TMJTWTFNAG), lectures 1–11 and their identified readings; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), the complete quantum primer and atomic-structure unit.

A wavefunction represents a quantum state under a specified model; its squared magnitude supports probability predictions with the appropriate normalization and variables. An orbital is a one-electron state or function used in describing electronic structure. Spin introduces intrinsic angular momentum and additional state labels. A drawn orbital boundary represents a chosen visualization convention.

For the hydrogenic model, quantum numbers organize allowed states and their angular/radial structure. Many-electron atoms add electron interactions, the Pauli principle, and approximation. Learn the electron-configuration rules with their conditions and exceptions rather than treating a filling mnemonic as a universal derivation of measured energies.

Connect a configuration with observables such as ionization behavior, spectra, and magnetic response. Neutral atoms and ions can require different configurations; removing electrons is not always equivalent to reversing a memorized filling order. Energies and their ordering belong to the actual system under discussion.

**Work:** explain allowed quantum-number sets and count the corresponding states for simple shells/subshells. Construct selected atomic/ionic configurations and compare textbook exceptions with their physical interpretation. For an advanced treatment follow the hydrogenic derivation in MIT 5.112, identifying the mathematical assumptions and the role of normalization.

Use the first textbook for an accessible entrance, then read the full advanced course's electronic-structure lectures. Calculus and quantum mechanics deepen the theory in their own subjects; introduce the meaning of a derivative, probability density, and normalization where the derivation uses them. The chemical task is to explain which structural conclusions the model supports and how those conclusions connect to evidence.

**Continue:** [Explain periodic trends through structure and competing effects](Periodic-patterns-and-their-limits.lingua#r_1JPAXST3A4ZTBAZQWJKCJR8AZW).

